A1 Monkey Spike Count Dataset
=============================

File
----
A1_dataset_monkey.mat


Source and Reference
---------------------
The spike-count data were recorded from primary auditory cortex (A1) of a macaque monkey and originate from the experimental recordings described in:

Kayser, C., Montemurro, M. A., Logothetis, N. K., & Panzeri, S. (2009). Spike-phase coding boosts and stabilizes information carried by spatial and temporal spike patterns. Neuron, 61(4), 597–608. Elsevier.


Description
-----------
This dataset contains spike-counts from primary auditory cortex (A1) during repeated presentation of a naturalistic auditory stimulus, either in its original form or mixed with background noise at different levels.

Neural responses are represented as spike counts computed in discrete temporal bins (delta = 50 ms) within each trial. The dataset includes multiple recording sessions and four noise conditions.


Main Variable
-------------
data_all_electrodes_all_sessions_all_noiselevels

Nested MATLAB cell array containing spike-count data across noise levels and recording sessions.


Data Organisation
-----------------
The data are organised hierarchically as:

Noise level
 └── Recording session
      └── Spike count array [nNeurons × nTrials × nTimeBins]

MATLAB access pattern:
data_all_electrodes_all_sessions_all_noiselevels{noise_level}{session}


Noise Conditions
----------------
The outermost cell array indexes four noise conditions, in order:

1. No noise – Original, unmodified stimulus.
2. Low noise – Background noise mixed at 6 dB below the original sound.
3. Medium noise – Background noise mixed at the same level as the original sound.
4. High noise – Background noise mixed at 6 dB above the original sound.


Dimensions
----------
Each recording session contains a 3D numeric array with dimensions:
[nNeurons × nTrials × nTimeBins]


Temporal Structure and Spike Counts
-----------------------------------
Stimulus duration per trial: 20 s
Temporal bin width: 50 ms

Spike counts represent the number of spikes observed in each 50 ms bin for a given neuron and trial.
